[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, let me recall the finite analog. For a finite group of order $lp^n$ with [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$l < p$ primes, the number of Sylow $p$-subgroups $n_p$ divides $l$ and $n_p [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\equiv 1 \pmod{p}$. Since $l < p$, $n_p$ divides $l$ means $n_p \in \{1, l\}$. [0m
